    "created_at": "2022-05-06T16:54:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-05-06T16:54:00Z",
    "body": "I'll pile on here.\r\n\r\nOn the one hand, I like WHATWG's general preference for using normal words in preference to jargon and I don't think that the definition of `character` in Infra is \"wrong\". \r\n\r\nHowever, spec developers don't understand the term that well: my worry is that they'll pick up the definition and use it imprecisely. When I18N does reviews, we often find text talking about \"characters\" but the assumption behind it is slipping around between bytes, code units (which obviously can _be_ bytes), code points, and graphemes. By getting specs to be specific, we avoid headaches later. Note that I18N WG made a conscious choice not to export `character` in the [I18N Glossary](https://www.w3.org/TR/i18n-glossary/#c).",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/infra/issues/comments/1119812035/reactions",
